00:41:36,521 --> 00:41:42,521
Happily N'Ever After is a 2006 animated fantasy
adventure comedy film directed by Paul J.

12
00:45:23,478 --> 00:45:29,478
Bolger, produced by John H.

13
00:49:10,434 --> 00:49:16,434
Williams, and written by Rob Moreland.

14
00:52:57,391 --> 00:53:03,391
It is inspired by fairy tales of the Brothers Grimm and Hans Christian Andersen and
loosely based on the 1999 animated German television series Simsala Grimm.

15
00:56:44,347 --> 00:56:50,347
The title is the opposite of a stock phrase, happily ever after; the name
is contracted with an apostrophe between the N and the E.

16
01:00:31,304 --> 01:00:37,304
The film stars the voices of Sarah Michelle Gellar, Freddie Prinze, Jr., Andy Dick,
Wallace Shawn, Patrick Warburton, George Carlin, and Sigourney Weaver.

17
01:04:18,260 --> 01:04:24,260
The film was one of Carlin's final works before his death in 2008.

18
01:08:05,217 --> 01:08:11,217
Lionsgate theatrically released Happily N'Ever After in
the United States on January 5, 2007.

19
01:11:52,173 --> 01:11:58,173
The film was panned by critics and grossed $38 million worldwide against a
production budget of $47 million, becoming a box-office bomb.

20
01:15:39,130 --> 01:15:45,130
Despite its negative reviews and poor performance, it was followed by a direct-to-video sequel
in 2009 called Happily N'Ever After 2: Snow White—Another Bite @ the Apple.

21
01:19:26,086 --> 01:19:32,086
The film explores themes of princess, dwarf,
wolf, fairy tale, bad mother-in-law.

22
01:23:13,043 --> 01:23:19,043
The tone is lighthearted.
